egismoc: report a busy device instead of aborting on a pending task

egismoc_open() asserted that no task state machine was pending:

    g_assert (self->task_ssm == NULL);

but nothing guarantees that. task_ssm is cleared in exactly one place,
egismoc_task_ssm_done(), which runs only when a state machine *completes*.
Neither teardown path clears it:

    egismoc_cancel()  cancels self->interrupt_cancellable, nothing else
    egismoc_close()   egismoc_cancel() + g_clear_object() + release_interface()

So an operation cancelled without its in-flight transfer callback running
leaves task_ssm set, the device is released, and the next claim reaches the
assertion with task_ssm != NULL. glib turns that into abort(), which is a bad
trade: fprintd dies with SIGABRT and every consumer of the reader -- PAM for
sudo, polkit, the lock screen -- loses fingerprint authentication until the
daemon restarts.

Reported upstream in TenSeventy7/libfprint-egismoc-sdcp#13, where two
reporters on different Egis sensors (1c7a:0582, and a Galaxy Book NP960XGL)
hit it through ordinary concurrent use of one reader by several PAM stacks.
egismoc_open(), egismoc_close() and egismoc_cancel() are byte-identical in
upstream libfprint 1.94.100, so this is not specific to the SDCP fork.

Report FP_DEVICE_ERROR_BUSY instead. pam_fprintd already copes with a busy
device, so the failure becomes a normal, recoverable one.

This deliberately does not clear the stale task_ssm -- see the in-code comment
for why that cannot be done safely from close() -- so the device stays busy
for the lifetime of the process. Still strictly better than aborting: no core
dump, no half-finished operation, and a daemon restart recovers it.

The interface claimed just above is released on this path, matching the other
early returns in the function.

--- a/libfprint/drivers/egismoc/egismoc.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/egismoc/egismoc.c
@@ -1904,7 +1904,34 @@
       return;
     }
 
-  g_assert (self->task_ssm == NULL);
+  /* A state machine from a previous operation can still be pending here.
+   * task_ssm is only cleared by egismoc_task_ssm_done(), i.e. on SSM
+   * completion; egismoc_cancel() and egismoc_close() both leave it set.  An
+   * operation cancelled before its transfer callback ran therefore survives
+   * the close and is still recorded when the device is claimed again.
+   *
+   * Do not abort over it.  g_assert() here kills fprintd with SIGABRT and
+   * takes fingerprint authentication down for every consumer of the reader
+   * until the daemon restarts.  A busy error is recoverable; pam_fprintd
+   * already handles one.
+   *
+   * Clearing the pending SSM from close() would be the fuller fix, but it
+   * cannot be done blindly: the completion callbacks differ per operation
+   * (egismoc_dev_init_done() calls fpi_device_open_complete(), while
+   * egismoc_task_ssm_done() calls fpi_device_action_error()), so finishing a
+   * stale SSM during close would signal completion for an action libfprint
+   * no longer considers active.
+   */
+  if (self->task_ssm != NULL)
+    {
+      g_usb_device_release_interface (fpi_device_get_usb_device (device),
+                                      0, 0, NULL);
+      fpi_device_open_complete (device,
+                                fpi_device_error_new_msg (FP_DEVICE_ERROR_BUSY,
+                                                          "Device is busy with a pending task"));
+      return;
+    }
+
   self->task_ssm = fpi_ssm_new (device, egismoc_dev_init_handler, DEV_INIT_STATES);
   fpi_ssm_start (self->task_ssm, egismoc_dev_init_done);
 }
